{"name":"Fennel Flower","hex":"#77aaff","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fennel flower","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of nigella (“seed used as a spice”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fennel_flower"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fennel","blocks":[{"text":"Fennel is a flowering plant species in the carrot family. It is a hardy, perennial herb with yellow flowers and feathery leaves. It is indigenous to the shores of the Mediterranean but has become widely naturalized in many parts of the world, especially on dry soils near the sea coast and on riverbanks."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Foeniculum_July_2011-1a.jpg/330px-Foeniculum_July_2011-1a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fennel"},{"title":"Flower","blocks":[{"text":"Flowers, also known as blossoms and blooms, are the reproductive structures of flowering plants. Typically, they are structured in four circular levels around the end of a stalk. These include: sepals, which are modified leaves that support the flower; petals, often designed to attract pollinators; male stamens, where pollen is presented; and female gynoecia, where pollen is received and its movement is facilitated to the egg. When flowers are arranged in a group, they are known collectively as an inflorescence."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg/330px-Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Fenouil.jpg/250px-Fenouil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Fenouil.jpg/500px-Fenouil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=par